                    briefing_text_r2:       `Architect the ~<strong>€2.94M</strong> of variable pay. Choose the financial-vs-ESG split (90/10, 80/20, 70/30, 60/40) — too little is immaterial and fails again; too much on soft targets invites a greenwashing charge and demotivates the CEO. Then decide how much of the ESG weight sits in the <strong>long-term incentive</strong> versus the annual bonus: a 2030 climate target is inherently long-term and belongs in the LTI, not a 12-month window. Finally, make ESG a <strong>standalone weighted component</strong> — visible and auditable — rather than a modifier that only tweaks what financials already earned.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose indicators & cap incentive intensity`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Two linked decisions. Pick a small, <strong>verifiable</strong> ESG indicator set: <em>absolute Scope 1+2</em> is the number investors track and your 2030 target rests on — intensity can fall while absolute emissions rise, and self-assessed culture or engagement won't survive a CSRD audit. Then set the <strong>payout cap</strong> (150/175/200%), add a <strong>financial gate</strong> so ESG never pays out in a poor financial year, and add <strong>malus &amp; clawback</strong>. Stress-test for gaming: could the CEO hit the metric without real progress?`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the policy & recover the vote`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The redesign meets resistance. The CEO's advisor says realistic payout dropped ~30% and a competitor is circling — lose the CEO and you trade a governance win for a leadership crisis. Yet one large investor still calls the ESG weighting too soft. Decide which feedback to accommodate and which to hold: the credibility test is <strong>verifiability and the gate</strong>, not raw weight. Pre-socialize a full <em>CSRD-aligned disclosure</em> with the bloc, then project the AGM vote and the long-term value signal it sends.`,

