                    coach_default:          `Decision approved.`,
                    coach_r1_root_right:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> The vote failed on token ESG and weak pay-for-performance alignment — 5% on €2.94M is immaterial, and financial pay barely tracks the results it rewards. Now you can fix the architecture, not the prose.`,
                    coach_r1_disclosure:    `<strong>This isn't a copy-editing problem.</strong> A better-written report won't move proxy advisors who object to the substance: a 5% ESG modifier they already called window-dressing. The policy itself has to change.`,
                    coach_r1_toomuchpay:    `<strong>Cutting the number isn't what they asked for.</strong> Investors didn't say pay less — they said link pay credibly to the 2030 targets. Slash the total and you demotivate the CEO who drove the turnaround without fixing the alignment that failed the vote.`,
                    coach_r1_investors:     `<strong>This isn't an activist ambush you can wait out.</strong> Two of your three largest holders and both proxy advisors are aligned. 'Hold the line' is how a 68% becomes a vote against the chair next year.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named token ESG and misalignment AND defined credibility as material weight on verifiable, CSRD-audited climate metrics in the LTI. That is exactly the brief that wins the vote without breaking incentives.`,
                    coach_r1_bump:          `<strong>5% to 10% is still window-dressing.</strong> At 10% of €2.94M that's roughly €294,000 on a transition that defines your equity story — barely material, and the advisors already told you 5% wasn't. You'll fail the vote again.`,
                    coach_r1_soft:          `<strong>Bold weight, soft metrics — the worst combination.</strong> Sustainability-culture and engagement scores are self-assessed and won't survive a CSRD audit. You'd be telling investors the CEO earns ESG pay on numbers no one can verify.`,
                    coach_r1_nochange:      `<strong>No change is the one option that guarantees the crisis.</strong> Explaining the same policy more clearly is exactly what got you to 68%. The committee can no longer get away with that.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>Protecting the CEO over naming the gap is how policies fail twice.</strong> If the committee won't confront that financial pay barely tracks results, it will tweak the report and call it a redesign. Name the misalignment.`,
                    coach_r2_token:         `<strong>10% is still token.</strong> About €294,000 of a €2.94M package on the 2030 thesis — enough to fail the vote again, not enough to change behaviour. The 22% bloc wants material linkage.`,
                    coach_r2_norm:          `<strong>20% is the emerging norm and clears the materiality bar.</strong> Around €588,000 is a real signal. Push to 30% if you want the bloc fully behind you — and make sure the metrics underneath are verifiable.`,
                    coach_r2_material:      `<strong>30% is materially persuasive.</strong> About €882,000 tied to the transition is a number investors take seriously — provided the metrics are auditable and the package stays winnable for the CEO. Now mind retention.`,
                    coach_r2_overweight:    `<strong>40% looks bold but destabilizes the package.</strong> You've cut the financial drive that fuelled the turnaround and put 40% of variable pay at risk — the CEO's advisor will warn of demotivation and a retention risk. Materiality, not maximalism.`,
                    coach_r2_ltiright:      `<strong>This is well-architected.</strong> A material weight, mostly in the long-term incentive where a 2030 target belongs, as a standalone auditable component. The metric's horizon now matches the behaviour it should drive.`,
                    coach_r2_stiload:       `<strong>You've loaded a 2030 target into the annual bonus.</strong> Cutting absolute emissions at a cement maker is a multi-year capital project — judging it in a 12-month window invites short-term tricks and punishes long lead-time investment. Move it to the LTI.`,
                    coach_r3_absolute:      `<strong>Absolute Scope 1+2 is the right spine.</strong> It's the number your 2030 target and your investors actually rest on. Unlike intensity, it can't fall while total emissions rise — so a full ESG bonus can't coincide with rising emissions.`,
                    coach_r3_intensity:     `<strong>Intensity is the easy-metric trap.</strong> Carbon per tonne improves automatically as volumes grow, so absolute emissions can rise while you pay a full ESG bonus. Your 2030 target is absolute — that's the metric the thesis rests on.`,
                    coach_r3_culture:       `<strong>A self-assessed culture score won't survive a CSRD audit.</strong> It's exactly the unverifiable, gameable metric that turns ESG pay into a greenwashing headline. Choose data the company already reports externally.`,
                    coach_r3_engagement:    `<strong>A self-reported engagement index is unauditable.</strong> Under CSRD your ESG pay metrics must be the same verified data you report externally. This one isn't — drop it for a third-party-validated target.`,
                    coach_r3_verifset:      `<strong>A small, verifiable set — done right.</strong> Absolute emissions plus a revenue-share or SBTi-validated milestone are auditable under CSRD and within the CEO's line of sight. This is what survives the proxy-advisor lens.`,
                    coach_r3_capgate:       `<strong>Verifiable metrics, a sensible cap and a financial gate.</strong> You've limited windfalls and ensured ESG can't pay out in a year earnings miss and the share price falls. That's the answer to 'paid for promises while shareholders lost money'.`,
                    coach_r3_nocap:         `<strong>No cap is a windfall and an optics disaster.</strong> In a strong year the CEO banks an uncapped payout; in a weak one the press writes the story. A cap limits the windfall and protects the policy's credibility.`,
                    coach_r3_nogate:        `<strong>Without a financial gate, ESG pays out in a bad year.</strong> Miss earnings, drop the share price, and the CEO still banks a large ESG bonus — 'paid for promises while shareholders lost money'. Gate the ESG payout on financial performance.`,
                    coach_r4_rebalance:     `<strong>That's the two-sided answer.</strong> You held the verifiable metrics and the cap that win investors, but eased the stretch so the target is winnable — credible to the market AND motivating to the person executing the strategy.`,
                    coach_r4_capitulate:    `<strong>You've capitulated to the CEO.</strong> Cutting ESG weight and lifting the cap keeps the CEO happy and hands the proxy advisors their headline back. You traded the vote for a quiet life — and you'll lose both.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the retention risk is how a governance win becomes a leadership crisis.</strong> Lose the CEO who drove the five-year turnaround and the vote you won is worth little. Make it credible AND winnable.`,
                    coach_r4_verifiability: `<strong>You've located the credibility test correctly.</strong> It's verifiable absolute targets and the gate, not raw weight, that satisfy investors who know greenwashing when they see it. Holding firm here is what recovers the vote.`,
                    coach_r4_maxweight:     `<strong>Conceding raw weight isn't the same as credibility.</strong> Pushing ESG higher on whatever metrics they name risks the very greenwashing charge you're trying to avoid — and tips the CEO toward the exit. Hold firm on verifiability instead.`,
                    coach_r4_dropgate:      `<strong>Dropping the gate to look committed backfires.</strong> ESG that always pays — even in a bad financial year — is precisely the 'paid for promises' optics investors punish. The gate is part of what makes the policy credible.`,
                    coach_r4_minimal:       `<strong>Minimal disclosure leaves the vote to chance.</strong> SRD II and CSRD expect a comparable, auditable policy, and the bloc wants to see the detail before the AGM. Pre-socialize it or risk a second surprise.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>A polished narrative light on verification reads as spin.</strong> Investors who called 5% window-dressing will see through ambition without audit detail. Disclose targets, source, cap, gate and clawback — substance, not story.`,

